  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1007 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nA rather benign weather pattern holds through the forecast\nperiod. Currently, a shortwave trough is swinging across the\nGreat Lakes region. This shortwave is sending a weak cold front\nsouth through the area. Other than broken high clouds and winds\nshifting to the north, little sensible weather change is\nanticipated. Temperatures start in the 40s on Sunday, despite\nthe cold front. Surface high pressure settles over the region by\nafternoon and sunny skies return as well. High temperatures in\nthe 60s offer a mild, pleasant Sunday afternoon.\n\nWith high pressure and clear skies, temperatures fall into the\n30s and 40s to start the approaching work week. By Monday \nafternoon, a cut-off system over the Southern Plain and another \ntrough approaching the Great Lakes pushes yet another cold front\ntowards the region. Ahead of the front, southwest surface winds\nand sunny skies push most locations into the upper 60s to near \n70. \n\nSlightly cooler temperatures on Tuesday fill in behind the\naforementioned cold front. A gradual temperature decrease is \nforecast through the middle of the week as a large trough \ndeepens over the eastern half of the United States. This keeps \nnortherly winds over the area, high temperatures in the 50s and \nlow temperatures in the 30s are to be expected during the middle\nof the week as a result. The coldest temperatures are set to \narrive Friday morning. Freezing temperatures are possible, but \nOmaha may remain just above freezing. If Omaha does not reach \nfreezing, this would mean the latest freeze on record is almost \ninevitable.\n\nBy the end of week, temperatures begin to slowly increase again\nas upper-level ridging and southerly winds take over. High\ntemperatures in the 60s and low temperatures in the 40s return.\nRain remains elusive through the end of the forecast period.",
